  "notes_for_editor": "Disagreements stated in text: first tower date (1800 vs 1801), brick tower 1811 vs 1812, keepers' dwelling fire 1968 (LighthouseFriends) vs 1958 (SC Maritime Museum), lens order (fourth per LighthouseFriends vs fifth per museum article). Much of the narrative rests on LighthouseFriends (paraphrased) and a reprinted newspaper article, with primary confirmation only for the 1893 storm, 1899 telephone, 1916 dwelling move, 1922 Phoebe Crosby aid and 1925 fire/O'Hagan notice. No keeper details from newspapers (Chronicling America search found no hits). The roster was copied from the USLHS archive print (Principal Keeper, 1st Assistant, USCG); Grisillo death date from LighthouseFriends citing the Georgetown Times, 29 Dec 1933, not read directly. Bulletin 1925 fire item is dated only \"June 27\"; year inferred from the 1925 volume; OCR of the 1925 and 1916 Bulletins interleaves columns. Phoebe Crosby date within the fiscal year unknown; Palmetto/Grissillo spelling from the report. Heights: 87 ft = 26.5 m (NRHP and LF); the first-lit and focal height are left out. NRHP text OCR notes the island is boat-only as of 1973. Access wording for tower inferred (no source says it is open). Not read: USCG Historian page (archive blocked), SC Picture Project (read; not cited), Wikipedia. Checked 2026-10-27: 1925 fire item corrected (Delco light and store room of Navy radio compass station, 27 June; Bulletin issue covers July 1925, pp. 89-90); 1899 telephone line is fiscal year 1899; lens-order 'conflict' removed because LighthouseFriends also says fifth-order for the lens now in the museum (removal date 1986 vs museum article 1999); Grisillo death is second-hand via LighthouseFriends. USLHS roster differs from LighthouseFriends for Leonard P. Jackson (1939, LF only) and Spencer's end year (1947 LF, 1948 USLHS); roster follows USLHS.",
